.he "tty (1)                      9/30/26                      tty (1)"
.fo "                              -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
tty
- get terminal name
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
tty [ -s ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Tty
is a simple program that attempts to look up the full path name
and number of the current "tty" device (teletypewriter or terminal).
Normally, this is the name of the Micronix (terminal) port you logged
in on.
.sp
To ascertain your device number, enter the
.bd
tty
command.  A typical response is
.sp
.ti +5
/dev/ttyB
.sp
meaning that you are on "ttyB".
.sp
If no associated file name can be found, the message "not a tty" is
printed instead, and the exit status is 1.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-s	Print nothing at all.  The exit status still says whether the
standard input is a terminal, so a shell script can test it without
printing the name.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/dev - Directory in which to search for file names.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is v7's tty, which is fourteen lines, and the interface above is
the one the 1982 page describes.  The 1.61 binary differs from its own
page and this port follows the page: that binary prints "?" and exits
2 when it cannot name the terminal, and it answers -s exactly as it
answers nothing.
.sp
Ttyname is not in this tree's library.  There is a page for it -
ttyname (3) - and no code, so tty carries v7's ttyname with it.  A
descriptor is asked first whether it is a terminal, with isatty (3),
and is then looked up in /dev by device and inode.  That is what the
1982 tty does too - its syscall trace is open("/dev/") and a stat of
every name in the directory - so the mechanism is this program's and
not just the message.
.sp
The same routine sits in login, which uses it to find the terminal to
record in /etc/utmp; see login (1).  Login counts the directory
position as it goes - that is ttyslot - and hands back the bare name
"ttyA", because that is what a utmp line holds.  Tty wants the path
"/dev/ttyA" the page shows, and that is what ttyname returns here.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
login (1), ps (1), stty (1), ttyname (3)
